With regards to your message at 07:30 AM 10/12/01, Lars Brinkhoff. Where 
you stated:
>Lukas Pustina <lukas-mailinglist-account@pustina.net> writes:
> > sorry to bother with this kind of question. But I want to upgrade
> > the memory of my LX164.
>
>Likewise, I have an Alpha which is a little short on memory.  There is
>a chip on the motherboard labelled "EB66", and there are four memory
>slots.  There are three ISA slots two PCI slots, and the processor
>runs at 233 MHz.  My searches through FAQs and HOWTOs are
>inconclusive, but I think this is the type of memory I would need:
>
>         * 72-pin SIMM
>         * max 70 ns
>         * true ECC
>
>Does this seem to be right?  What is the maximum memory configuration?
Correct. True ECC means 9 chips per bank, not simulated parity.
If I recall correctly it will take up to 128MB modules.
Pricing on these is now a bit high, as they are not commonly used any more.
For example, 128MB ( 4 x 32MB) is now worth around $200 USD.


