I am now downloading a kernel while I type this.
Transfer rate is sticking at around 168K/sec.
The physical connection is DSL 4.0 Mb/sec.

What is the limiting factor in most of these cases is the bandwidth of your 
service provider, divided by the user activity eating it up. This is 
especially pronounced on cable modems, where the number of subscribers 
sharing a fixed backbone/switching bandwidth is pretty severe.
Even among DSL providers on the same copper pairs and gateways, we see as 
much as a 3 fold variance in speed..

Just because you have a metered physical link at 1.5Mbps, that doesn't mean 
the service provider has enough infrastructure bandwidth to give you that 
speed. This is the "great lie" of broadband, especially cable modem style..
