That's a very good question.  How do you measure how much stack you need?  When I
first started to learn sysadmin, I was told - "Our software will crash unless
stacksize is set to unlimited".  I just accepted that as fact.  I do know that on
the alpha with 2.2.16 running, a stacksize of 32768 was enough to not cause the
machine to lock up, but it wasn't enough to prevent the software from crashing if
it required more than 128MB of RAM.

Ken

jsturm1@home.com wrote:

> On Tue, 16 Oct 2001, Ken Siersma wrote:
> > Wow.  So, what do you run?  I was very happy to see that RH 7.1 could handle a
> > stacksize set to unlimited.  RH 6.2 couldn't do that.  Do you know if running
> > a 2.2.x kernel could affect 7.1's ability to do this?  The ability to handle
> > unlimited stacksize was the most important reason I upgraded to 7.1.
>
> How much stack do you need?  I had no trouble with a 512MB stack and
> a 2.2.19 kernel (after I did "ulimit -s unlimited" as root).
>
> Note that the kernel initializes the stack bottom to 0x120000000 so it
> could not grow much larger than 4GB regardless, unless you allocate an
> alternate stack (e.g. with mmap).
>
